# GETPID(2)

## NAME

getpid, getppid - get process identification

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <unistd.h>

pid_t getpid(void);

pid_t getppid(void);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getpid* returns the process ID of the current process; *getppid* that of its parent. Most
often it is used to generate uniquely-named temporary files.

## SEE ALSO

mktemp(3), fork(2)

## BESM-6 NOTES

**One extracode, two entry points.** The gate returns the process id in the accumulator and the
*parent's* in r12, so *getpid* and *getppid* are two labels on one stub - which is why it is
hand-written rather than generated. Getuid(2) and getgid(2) have the same shape.

r12 is an index register and so only 15 bits wide. That does not bite here - process ids are
small - but it does in wait(2).

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_getpid** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
